# Python
backend/.venv/
backend/.venv-train/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# Model weights (large; download separately — see requirements-ml.txt)
backend/weights/*.pt
*.pt
backend/yolo*.pt
backend/weights/*.pth.tar
# EXCEPT weights trained in-house on our GPU — these are NOT re-downloadable;
# losing them means losing the training runs (see HANDOFF.md §4).
!backend/weights/ballnet.pt
!backend/weights/ballnet_v0.pt
!backend/weights/ballnet_v2.pt
!backend/weights/ballnet_pre_visweighted.pt
!backend/weights/ballnet_v21.pt
!backend/weights/ballnet_v3.pt
!backend/weights/courtnet_ft.pt
!backend/weights/courtnet_ft_v0_notready.pt
# The LEAK-CLEAN court fine-tune (Aug 2026), trained on data/gold/court_split.json.
# `external-research-reconciled.md` calls it "the ONLY eligible fine-tune" - every
# other court checkpoint was trained on a pool that overlaps the gold set (trap T06).
# It was untracked until 2026-09-11 and existed on exactly one disk; a cleanup pass
# listed it for deletion as if it were a third-party download, which is how an
# irreproducible training run nearly went. `court_detector.pt` beside it IS upstream's
# Jun 2023 release and stays ignored.
!backend/weights/courtnet_split.pt
# Session I's two 15-epoch A/B arms. NOT SHIPPABLE — undertrained by design, and
# they score 14-15 solid ghosts against ballnet_v21's 9. Kept because they were
# trained BEFORE `--seed` existed, so they can never be reproduced, and every
# number in data/output/session_i_ab/results.md is measured on these exact files.
!backend/weights/ballnet_i_base.pt
!backend/weights/ballnet_i_conf.pt

# Session K's data-volume A/B arms (2026-08-13). Both `--seed 0` and recipe-stamped,
# so unlike Session I's they ARE reproducible in principle — but only from
# data/ball_dataset, which is gitignored and has no off-machine copy, and only at
# 1h16m / 1h52m of GPU. `pool_new_s0` is the +57%-data arm and the highest detector
# recall this project has recorded (82.2% on the legacy six vs v21's 69.4%); it is a
# SHIPPING CANDIDATE, not shipped — the chain test has not been run. Every number in
# data/output/pool_ab.md is measured on these exact files.
!backend/weights/pool_old_s0.pt
!backend/weights/pool_new_s0.pt

# Exported mobile models (regenerate with v2/mobile/export_tracknet.py).
# Path updated 2026-09-11 when mobile/ was shelved to v2/ - the old rule stopped
# matching and a `git add -A` committed 73 MB of binaries that had been excluded
# on purpose. Both spellings kept so an old checkout still ignores them.
mobile/models/*.onnx
mobile/models/*.onnx.data
mobile/node_modules/
v2/mobile/models/*.onnx
v2/mobile/models/*.onnx.data
v2/mobile/node_modules/

# Node / frontend
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/dist/
.vite/

# Data & analysis output (keep the demo sample_match.json tracked)
data/output/*
!data/output/.gitkeep
# EXCEPT JSON outputs — small, and they are the EVIDENCE for the unresolved
# ball-track regression (HANDOFF.md §6): archived perception caches + match
# outputs must be recoverable. Videos/images in data/output stay ignored.
!data/output/*.json
# ...EXCEPT the P0 resolution-sweep perception caches. The rule above was written
# for caches that were small; these are 9-14 MB each, 74 MB across 23 files, and
# git history cannot be slimmed afterwards without a rewrite. Their FINDINGS are
# recorded (P0-2's downscaling failure, P0-3's upscale-factor lever); the per-frame
# arrays are reproducible from the tools that made them. Regenerate, do not archive.
data/output/p0_*.perception.json
# Same reasoning for the eval-ladder .txt results. CLAUDE.md cites
# data/output/gold_v21_e6.txt BY NAME (the Session F score sweep reproduces it
# digit for digit), so leaving them out meant the repo cited a result it did not
# contain. Small, text, and they are the evidence.
!data/output/*.txt
# ...and written-up results. data/output/court_consensus_bar.md is cited BY NAME in
# CLAUDE.md, and the repo has already shipped one commit citing evidence it did not
# contain. Same rule: a result nobody can reproduce isn't one.
!data/output/*.md
# Same rule one level down for the Session E1 fps-controlled experiment: the
# swept perception caches ARE the evidence for the "fps buys precision, not
# recall" result (HANDOFF.md §13) and must stay reproducible. Images still ignored.
!data/output/fps/
!data/output/fps/*.json
# Same rule for the false-fire inspection: the per-model locks.json files ARE the
# evidence (every lock's frame, image position, court position and class), they are
# ~40 KB each, and data/output/false_fires.md cites them. The contact sheets and the
# 5.8 MB viewer page are DERIVED — regenerate with tools/false_fire_viewer.py from
# these JSONs plus data/gold/frames — so images stay ignored as everywhere else.
!data/output/false_fires/
!data/output/false_fires/*/
!data/output/false_fires/*/*.json
# NOTE the re-ignores below are load-bearing. `data/output/*` only matches ONE
# level, so un-ignoring the false_fires directory makes git descend and pick up
# EVERYTHING under it — the first attempt staged 59 MB of contact sheets and a
# 5.8 MB viewer page. Un-ignoring a directory is not the same as un-ignoring a
# file type inside it.
data/output/false_fires/**/*.png
data/output/false_fires/**/*.jpg
data/output/false_fires/**/*.html
data/output/false_fires/**/*.mp4
data/output/fps/*.png
# Re-including the fps/ directory above also un-ignored the rendered videos it
# holds (46 MB of *.annotated.mp4). Videos stay ignored one level down too —
# they are regenerable from the JSON caches with `run.py analyze --annotate`.
data/output/fps/*.mp4
# Session I's A/B evidence, same one-level-down rule as fps/ above. `data/output/*`
# excludes the DIRECTORY, and git will not descend into an excluded directory, so the
# `!data/output/*.json` negation never reaches files inside a subfolder of it.
!data/output/session_i_ab/
!data/output/session_i_ab/*.json
!data/output/session_i_ab/*.md
# Contact sheets stay out, same as fps/*.png: the classification is the evidence and
# it lives in the JSON. Regenerate a sheet with inspect_false_locks --contact-sheet.
data/output/session_i_ab/*.png
# ...and the one evidence image from the 2026-07-05 regression investigation.
!data/output/regression_diff.jpg
data/*.mp4
data/*.mov

# Raw training media + derived datasets (large; regenerable — clips re-download
# from YouTube ids in HANDOFF.md §4, datasets rebuild via the labeler scripts)
data/train_clips/
data/amateur_clips/
# Videos promoted to the exam set. Same policy as the training clips: large,
# and the repo tracks the LABELS and the calibration, never the media. Note
# data/*.mp4 above does NOT cover a subdirectory, which is how 1.4 GB of
# video nearly went in with a gold promotion.
data/gold_clips/
data/ball_dataset/
data/court_dataset/
# The COURT clips held out as TEST (data/gold/court_split.json). Same policy as
# court_dataset: the frames are regenerable, the SPLIT MANIFEST is committed.
data/court_testset/

# Gold-label frame extracts (regenerable from the source video via
# tools/select_gold_frames.py --extract-only; the manifest + labels JSON in
# data/gold/ ARE committed — they are the human benchmark, HANDOFF §8 fix 1.4)
data/gold/frames/
# Same split for the TRAIN pool: the human CLICKS are the irreplaceable part and
# are committed; the frame copies are not, because select_farcourt_labels.py
# re-extracts them from the source videos in data/train_clips/ (which are
# themselves untracked — see the backup note in SCOREBOARD).
data/labels/frames/

# Throwaway probe scripts from a working session (a real tool belongs in tools/)
scratchpad/

# Editor / OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/

# Downloaded full match/highlight videos (user-sourced test footage; ~GBs,
# regenerable from YouTube by id in the filename - never belongs in git)
data/highlights/
data/output/synth/*.npz

# Lab (tools/lab_server.py) job artifacts. Same policy as data/output: the JSON
# evidence IS committed — an eval scored against human gold is a result, and a
# result nobody can reproduce isn't one — but the raw subprocess logs are bulky
# and regenerable, so they stay out. data/clips/ (the train-vs-gold declaration
# for each clip) is deliberately NOT ignored: it is the record of which footage
# is benchmark and which is training data.
data/runs/*.log
# Source videos (6.6 GB) stay out of git, but the layout README does NOT: it
# records which files are GOLD test footage, and that is the only warning a human
# gets now that gold videos sit beside training footage in the surface folders.
# A directory rule would stop git descending, so exclude the CONTENTS and re-admit
# the README.
data/incoming/*
!data/incoming/README.md

# Per-rally highlight clips (run.py highlights) are generated artifacts, same
# policy as data/output: the mp4s are ignored, the manifest is evidence.
frontend/public/rallies/*
!frontend/public/rallies/highlights.json

# yt_match40 is a 6-min clip, so its perception cache is 11 MB — 3.5x the largest
# cache otherwise tracked. Evidence caches ARE normally committed (CLAUDE.md), but
# this clip is a highlights DEMO, not a tuning target: the 511 KB match.json is the
# artifact anything downstream reads. Re-make it with `run.py analyze` if needed.
data/output/yt_match40.perception.json

# eval/ — the court-detection feedback loop. The harness and the drop-zone are
# tracked; the rendered overlays are regenerable output (run eval/run_eval.py).
eval/out/

eval/sheets/
eval/verdicts/

# The extracted eval frames are regenerable output (eval/collect_frames.py is
# deterministic: uniform positions, then the dominant-view cluster). The README
# and eval/clip_classes.json - the human court-in-view verdicts - stay tracked.
eval/frames/*/

# Derived scratch from the eval harness. The `_` prefix is this repo's existing
# convention for "regenerable, do not commit": extracted frame sets, clean-plate
# galleries, fit caches, traces. eval/_shell_gallery alone is 22 MB of 4K JPEGs
# that `tools/make_label_plates.py` rebuilds in one command, and eval/_candidates
# is 25 MB of extracted frames. The evidence they produce (data/output/*.json,
# *.md) IS committed - the intermediates are not.
eval/_*
data/output/_*

# transient concurrent-agent slots (see .claude/hooks/agent-cap.sh)
.claude/.agent-locks/
.claude/.agent-queue/
.claude/.agent-reservations/
.claude/.agent-spend.log
